        {
          "_id": "64c0e8326f5bfc7ab78aeb6e",
          "previousYearPapers": [
            {
              "_id": "64c0e679aef333185c6bb44d",
              "examId": "615d7713c52ffa3c944600e3",
              "heldOn": "2022-04-30T09:00:00.000Z",
              "isVisible": true,
              "shortName": "2022",
              "title": "BITSAT 2022"
            }
          ],
          "type": "singleCorrect",
          "level": 3,
          "question": {
            "text": "The given graph represents the variation of compressibility factor $(Z)=\\frac{p V}{n R T}$, for three real gases $A, B$ and $C$. Identify the only incorrect statement.\r\n<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/bitsat/oyRA5keONN_o_pg7YjXunRyP45aYxtUbrNo4PgoPv30.original.fullsize.png\"><br>",
            "image": null
          },
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "ynZRD",
              "text": "\r\nFor the gas $A, a=0$ and its dependence on $p$ is linear at all pressure.\r\n",
              "isCorrect": false,
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "8iNMQ",
              "text": "\r\nFor the gas $B, b=0$ and its dependence on $p$ is linear at all pressure.\r\n",
              "isCorrect": true,
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "BOvnE",
              "text": "\r\nFor the gas $C$, which is typical real gas for which neither. $a$ nor $b=0$. By knowing the minima and point of the intersection, with $Z=1, a$ and $b$ can be calculated.\r\n",
              "isCorrect": false,
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "5vA0X",
              "text": "\r\nAt high pressure the slope is positive for all real gases.",
              "isCorrect": false,
              "image": null
            }
          ],
          "correctValue": null,
          "solution": {
            "text": "From the graph it is clear that, the value of ' $Z$ ' decreases with increase of pressure. We can explain as follows on the basis of van der Waals' equation.\r\nAt high pressure, when ' $p$ ' is large, $V$ will be small and one cannot ignore ' $b$ ' in comparison to $V$. However, the team $a / V^2$ may be considered negligible in comparison to ' $p$ ' in van der Waals' equation.\r\n$\\left(p+\\frac{a}{V^2}\\right)(V-b)=n R T$\r\n$p(V-b)=n R T$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\quad p V-p b=n R T$\r\nor    $\\frac{p V}{n R T}=1+\\frac{p b}{n R T}$\r\nor $Z=1+\\frac{p b}{n R T}$\r\nThus, $Z$ is greater than 1. As pressure is increased (at constant $T$ ), the factor $\\frac{p b}{n R T}$ increases. This explains why after minima in the curves, $Z$ increase continuously with pressure. Hence, the only incorrect statement is (b).",
            "image": null
          },
          "videoSolution": null,
          "questionLabels": [],
          "syllabusCategory": "noChange",
          "questionStructure": [
            "default"
          ],
          "previousYear": {
